1. OS — Operating System

OS: Software that manages the computer's hardware, files, memory, and applications.
Example: Windows, macOS, Linux.

2. path Module

Path: Used to work with file and folder paths in Node.js.

Example:

path.join("folder", "file.txt")
3. fs — File System

fs: Used to create, read, update, and delete files/folders in Node.js.

Example:

fs.readFile()
fs.writeFile()
4. dns — Domain Name System

DNS: Converts a domain name into an IP address.

Example:

google.com → IP address

5. process

process: Gives information/control about the currently running Node.js program.

Important:

process.argv

process.argv → Used to take command-line arguments/input.

6. crypto

crypto: Used for encryption, hashing, and generating secure values.

Example:

Password hashing
Data encryption
Security-related operations

Hashing ≠ Encryption

Hashing: One-way → cannot normally be reversed.
Encryption: Can be decrypted using a key.
7. Node.js Modules

A module is a reusable piece of code that we can import and use.

Types of Modules

A. Core/Built-in Modules
Already provided by Node.js; no installation needed.

Examples:

fs
path
dns
http
crypto
process

B. External/Third-party Modules
Created by other developers and installed using npm.

Example:

npm install express

C. Internal/Local Modules
Modules/files created by you inside your project.

Example:

require("./math")
8. npm

npm = Node Package Manager

Used to:

Install packages
Manage dependencies
Create/manage Node.js projects
Basic setup

Step 1:

npm init -y

Creates package.json.

Step 2:

npm install is-even

Installs an external package.

Step 3: Import/use the package.

For CommonJS:

const isEven = require("is-even");

Step 4: Use it in your program.

9. CommonJS vs ES Modules

Two ways to work with modules in Node.js.

CommonJS

Uses:

require()
module.exports

Example:

const fs = require("fs");
ES Modules

Uses:

import
export

Example:

import fs from "fs";

Easy difference:

CommonJS → require

ES Module → import/export

10. CLI — Command Line Interface

CLI: Interface where we interact with a program using commands in the terminal.

Example:

node app.js

Node.js can also receive command-line input using:

process.argv
11. HTTP

HTTP = HyperText Transfer Protocol

It is a set of rules used for communication between client and server.

Simple:

Client → Request → Server

Server → Response → Client

Example:

Browser requests a webpage → Server sends response.

12. HTTP Methods

HTTP methods tell the server what action we want to perform.

Method	Meaning
GET	Get/read data
POST	Create/send new data
PUT	Completely update existing data
PATCH	Partially update existing data
DELETE	Delete data
PUT vs PATCH

PUT → Complete update

PATCH → Partial update

Example:

Existing user:

Name: Aprajita
Age: 19
City: Delhi

PUT → Send the complete updated user.

PATCH → Send only:

Age: 20
13. Browser & API Testing

A browser normally makes GET requests easily through the address bar.

For testing APIs with methods like:

GET
POST
PUT
PATCH
DELETE

we commonly use tools such as Postman.

Postman allows us to:

Send API requests
Add request body
Add headers
Test APIs
Check server responses

Important correction: Browsers are not limited to GET. JavaScript running in a browser can send POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, etc. The address bar itself is primarily for GET navigation.

14. JSON

JSON = JavaScript Object Notation

A lightweight, commonly used data-interchange format between client and server.

Example:

{
  "name": "Aprajita",
  "age": 19
}
JSON.stringify()

Object → JSON string

JSON.stringify(obj)
JSON.parse()

JSON string → JavaScript object

JSON.parse(data)

Easy trick:

Stringify → String

Parse → Object

15. Router

A Router is used to organize different API routes/endpoints.

Example:

/users
/products
/orders

Instead of putting everything in one file, we can separate routes.

16. Express.js

Express.js: A minimal and unopinionated web framework for Node.js, commonly used to create servers and REST APIs.

It makes handling:

Routes
Requests
Responses
Middleware
APIs

easier.

Example:

app.get("/users", (req, res) => {
    res.send("Users");
});
"Minimal" means:

It provides the basic tools without forcing a large structure.

"Unopinionated" means:

It does not force you to organize your application in one particular way.

17. minimist

minimist: An external npm package used to parse command-line arguments.

Example command:

node app.js --name Aprajita

It helps convert command-line arguments into an easier-to-use object.